Z||000000||Smoooooth GT||11-27-2001||08:36 PM||||Well...  Not really a explosion..lol  sounded more like a backfire.. Then I have NO SPARK coming from my Brand New MSD coil... I even tried my older Accel coil and still don't have any spark coming from it??<P>My question is...  What else could it be??  maybe something in my distributor?? The module in the bottom of it?? I have another dist. somewhere..lol I'll try to find it tomorrow... anyone know??  Thank's for the input!!      Steve<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smooooothgtsig_9.gif"> <BR>Shaved door handles, sidetrim, exhaust outlet's, etc.... smooooth!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroRacer||11-27-2001||08:42 PM||||Maybe you lost the distributor gear?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||11-27-2001||08:55 PM||||Haynes Manual: Engine backfires<P>1 Emission system not functioning<BR>2 Inition timing incorrect<BR>3 Faulty secondary ignition system (cracked sparkplug insulator, faulty plug wires, distributor cap and/or rotor)<BR>4 Fuel injection sytem in need of adjustment<BR>5 Vacuum leak at injectors<BR>6 Valves sticking<P>I'd look a #3.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gary W||11-27-2001||09:16 PM||||I'll bet it's the ignition module. Had the same thing happen on a Buick. Big Bang, then it wouldn't start.||||reg||1
Z||000004||jelly2m8||11-27-2001||10:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Gary W:<BR><B>I'll bet it's the ignition module. Had the same thing happen on a Buick. Big Bang, then it wouldn't start.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'd put my money on the ignition module also.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||11-27-2001||10:59 PM||||Yup. Ignition module. <BR>Just for grins, unplug the tach filter. They can cause trouble, too.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||ThatRickGuy||11-27-2001||11:04 PM||||my ignition modual went out while I was hauling allong I=95 last summer. suddenly the engine just cut out, after a few secs as as the car was slowing down, still in gear, it started working again and the engine started itself scince it was still spinning. this happened a few more times until I got to work. The stock ones hold up decent when you consider the constant heat soaking they take. it's only $30 or so for a new one and it could likely be your culprit.||||reg||1
Z||000007||grinthock||11-27-2001||11:05 PM||||could a flakey ignition module cause the car to run rich or just cut completly?  Maybe I am having this problem....<P>Where is the darn thing?||||reg||1
Z||000008||artherd||11-28-2001||05:14 AM||||HOLD UP DECENT?!?!?<P>They FAIL.  That is BAD!<P>They're all junk as far as I am concerned, a major Fiero design flaw.  NO SOLID STATE ELECTRONIC PART in an automobile should be a 'wear item' and need perodic replacement.  Bloody hell!<P>Jet engine motor actuator drives don't fail, and they take far more heat than we'll ever see.  If they failed, F14 pilots would be falling out of the skies.<P>A new one is over $100 from a GM dealer (don't even think of buying a knock-off, it'll die in a week.)<P>So like GM to build a great car then handicap it with a crap engine.  &lt;sigh&gt;.  They got (a few, TDC, Quad4, N*) motors right in later years.  Too bad we don't see them in many GM cars....<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fiero_24||11-28-2001||08:21 AM||||I am with them on the ignition module.  It happened to me before, and when I changed it there were no problems.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/driverside41.jpg"> <BR>  <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||6
Z||000010||StuGood||11-28-2001||09:37 AM||||Think unplugging the tach filter is the place to start (since it's a no-cost test).  Keep a new GM ignition module in the car (along with tools to change it out), and you'll have a (fairly) cheap insurance policy on board.<P>That's what Stu'd do, anyway.  Do what you want  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">     ||||reg||1
Z||000011||mshill||11-28-2001||01:32 PM||||If you now have no spark, I put money on the ignition module.  If you had consistent backfiring I would bet on the pickup coil in the distributor.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||V8Dreamer||11-28-2001||03:48 PM||||I'm going with the ign mod since I just had one go on me a week or so ago......and that one was just replaced about a year ago!!!!!!!!!!! It is located under the dist cap held to the base by 2 (3/16 I think) bolts.  Takes about 5 min to replace, and make sure you don't forget to apply the heat transfer grease to the bottom of the module before installing.....<P>-Ryan||||reg||1
